      "name": "__strcmpi",
      "signature": "int __strcmpi(char * lpszStr1, char * lpszStr2)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "int",
      "comment": "Case-insensitive string comparison (strcmpi implementation).\nCompares two null-terminated strings character-by-character, treating uppercase\nand lowercase ASCII letters as equivalent. Uses two code paths based on locale.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check g_nAnsiCodePage to determine comparison mode\n2. If code page is 0 (C locale / no locale):\n   a. Compare characters byte-by-byte until mismatch or null terminator\n   b. On mismatch, convert both chars to lowercase using fast ASCII math\n   c. If lowercase chars match, continue comparing\n   d. If still different, return comparison result (-1, 0, or 1)\n3. If code page is set (ANSI locale active):\n   a. Compare characters byte-by-byte until mismatch or null terminator\n   b. On mismatch, call _tolower() for locale-aware case conversion\n   c. Compare lowercased characters and continue if equal\n   d. Return comparison result based on final difference\n\nParameters:\n  lpszStr1 - Pointer to first null-terminated string to compare\n  lpszStr2 - Pointer to second null-terminated string to compare\n\nReturns:\n  Negative value (-1): lpszStr1 is less than lpszStr2 (case-insensitive)\n  Zero (0): Strings are equal (case-insensitive)\n  Positive value (1): lpszStr1 is greater than lpszStr2 (case-insensitive)\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n  0x41 - ASCII 'A' (uppercase letter start)\n  0xBF - Offset to normalize 'A' to 0 (0x41 + 0xBF = 0x100, wraps to 0)\n  0x1A - Number of letters in alphabet (26 decimal)\n  0x20 - Difference between uppercase and lowercase ASCII (32 decimal)\n  0xFF - Initial loop counter / non-null indicator\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Empty strings: Returns 0 (both are equal)\n  - Null terminator reached: Comparison stops, result based on current state\n  - Non-letter characters: Compared as-is without case conversion\n\nLibrary: Visual Studio 1998 Release",
      "name_source": "Classic/1.00",
