      "name": "__itoa",
      "signature": "char * __itoa(int nValue, char * szDest, int nRadix)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "char *",
      "comment": "Converts a signed integer to its string representation in the specified radix.\n\nStandard C library itoa implementation that handles signed/unsigned conversion\nbased on radix and value sign. For base-10 with negative values, prepends a\nminus sign; otherwise treats value as unsigned.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load radix parameter and value parameter from stack\n2. Check if radix equals 10 (decimal base)\n3. If radix is 10, test if value is negative (signed comparison)\n4. For negative decimal values: call ConvertUintToString with signed flag (1)\n5. For positive decimal or non-decimal radix: call ConvertUintToString with unsigned flag (0)\n6. Store destination pointer to return and clean up stack\n7. Return pointer to the converted string buffer\n\nParameters:\n  nValue - Signed integer value to convert to string representation\n  szDest - Pointer to destination buffer for the resulting string (must be large enough)\n  nRadix - Numeric base for conversion (2-36, typically 10 for decimal, 16 for hex)\n\nReturns:\n  char* - Pointer to szDest buffer containing the null-terminated string\n  Never returns NULL; caller must ensure buffer is adequately sized\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Negative values with radix 10: Converted with leading minus sign\n- Negative values with radix != 10: Treated as unsigned (two's complement representation)\n- Zero value: Returns \"0\" string\n\nMagic Numbers:\n- 0xa (10): Decimal radix check for signed number handling\n- 0x1: Flag passed to ConvertUintToString indicating signed negative conversion\n- 0x0: Flag passed to ConvertUintToString indicating unsigned conversion",
      "name_source": "Classic/1.00",
      "method": "NOP",
      "index": "NOP:6d978279eca943578172b437a1d6d3aa8114b05a1fdc36b03e8f591731c3b40a",
