      "name": "CopyMemoryBlock",
      "signature": "void * CopyMemoryBlock(void * pDest, void * pSrc, uint dwSize)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "void *",
      "comment": "Copies dwSize bytes from pSrc to pDest, handling overlapping memory regions.\nThis is an optimized implementation matching Visual Studio 1998 memcpy/memmove.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if source and destination overlap (pSrc < pDest && pDest < pSrc + dwSize)\n2. If overlapping, use backward copy to prevent data corruption\n3. If non-overlapping, use forward copy for better cache performance\n4. For both directions:\n   a. Check destination DWORD alignment (address & 3)\n   b. If aligned: Copy DWORDs with REP MOVSD, then handle 0-3 remainder bytes\n   c. If unaligned and size >= 12: Align destination first with byte copies\n   d. If unaligned and size < 12: Use byte-by-byte copy (MOVSB)\n5. Remainder bytes (dwSize & 3) handled via jump table dispatch\n\nParameters:\n  pDest  - void* destination buffer pointer (returned in EAX)\n  pSrc   - void* source buffer pointer\n  dwSize - uint number of bytes to copy\n\nReturns:\n  void* - Returns pDest (original destination pointer)\n  Always succeeds - no error checking on NULL pointers\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  Size 0: No copy performed, returns pDest immediately\n  Overlap: Backward copy ensures correct result when pSrc < pDest < pSrc + dwSize\n\nMagic Numbers:\n  0x3  - DWORD alignment mask (4 bytes - 1)\n  0xC  - Threshold (12 bytes) below which alignment optimization not worth cost\n  0x2  - Right shift to convert byte count to DWORD count (divide by 4)\n\nPerformance:\n  Uses REP MOVSD for bulk copy (1 DWORD per cycle on 486+)\n  Jump tables at 0x1000bae8 and 0x1000bb80 for remainder dispatch",
      "name_source": "Classic/1.00",
      "method": "NOP",
