      "name": "__isctype",
      "signature": "int __isctype(int nChar, int nTypeMask)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "int",
      "comment": "Tests whether a character belongs to a specified character type category.\n\nThis is a CRT library function that checks character classification against a\ntype mask. For ASCII characters (0-255), it performs a direct lookup in the\nglobal character type table. For multi-byte characters, it uses the Windows\nGetStringType API via ___crtGetStringTypeA to determine character type.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if character is in ASCII range (nChar + 1 < 0x101)\n2. If ASCII: Direct lookup in g_pCTypeTable, return masked result\n3. If multi-byte: Extract high byte to check lead byte flag\n4. If lead byte has 0x80 flag: Build 2-byte MBCS string (high,low,null)\n5. If not lead byte: Build 1-byte string (char,null)\n6. Call ___crtGetStringTypeA to get character type info\n7. Return type info masked with requested type mask, or 0 on failure\n\nParameters:\n  nChar     - Character code to test (int, may be multi-byte)\n  nTypeMask - Bitmask of character types to test against (int)\n              Uses CRT character type flags (_UPPER, _LOWER, _DIGIT, etc.)\n\nReturns:\n  Non-zero if character matches any of the specified types in nTypeMask\n  Zero if character does not match or if type lookup fails\n\nMagic Numbers:\n  0x100 - ASCII character range boundary (256 characters)\n  0x80  - Lead byte flag in character type table (indicates MBCS lead byte)\n  0x1   - Single-byte string length for ___crtGetStringTypeA\n  0x2   - Two-byte string length for MBCS characters\n  0xFFFF - Mask to extract lower 16 bits of character type info\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - EOF (-1): Returns 0 since -1 + 1 = 0 < 0x101, lookup at offset -2\n  - Multi-byte lead bytes: Builds proper MBCS string before type lookup\n  - Invalid MBCS sequences: Returns 0 from ___crtGetStringTypeA failure",
      "name_source": "Classic/1.00",
      "method": "NOP",
